<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="love">Jeremiyah Love: The Gold Standard</h2>
<p>No rookie carried a bigger combine number into 2026 than Notre Dame running back Jeremiyah Love. Love ran an official 4.36-second 40-yard dash at 212 pounds, matching the time Jahmyr Gibbs posted in 2023 despite Love carrying 15 more pounds. It was the fastest 40 among running backs with a 90-plus NGS production score in the last decade, and it pushed him into top-five draft conversations before Arizona ultimately made him the No. 3 overall pick.</p>
<div class="stat-card">
<div class="stat-icon">⚡</div>
<div class="stat-title">Jeremiyah Love</div>
<div class="stat-sub">RB · Arizona Cardinals · No. 3 Overall Pick</div>
<div class="stat-numbers">
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">4.36s</div><div class="label">40-Yard Dash</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">212 lbs</div><div class="label">Combine Weight</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">$53M</div><div class="label">Guaranteed (Rookie Deal)</div></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>The number was never really in question — it's what a player does with that speed between the tackles that separates workout warriors from difference-makers. Early camp reporting out of Arizona has been cautious rather than glowing: new offensive coordinator Nathaniel Hackett has pointed to incumbent James Conner's experience over Love's rookie status when discussing the starting job, even while acknowledging Love's traits. That's a normal camp dynamic — veterans hold the job until a rookie takes it — but it's worth watching whether Love's timed speed shows up as functional playmaking speed once pads come on.</p>
