<h2 id="thompson">Brenen Thompson: The Combine's Fastest Man</h2>
<p>Mississippi State receiver Brenen Thompson didn't just win his position group at the 2026 combine — he ran the fastest 40 of any player at the event, 4.26 seconds. Straight-line speed like that translates most directly on special teams and in vertical route concepts, and camp beat reporters will be watching whether it shows up as separation against NFL press coverage, not just cleared air on a track.</p>
<p>Speed scores are notoriously position-dependent. A 4.26 means something different for a slot receiver working option routes than it does for an outside "X" who needs to stack a cornerback on a go route. That distinction is exactly why raw 40 times get overrated in draft coverage and underrated in camp coverage, where route-running and hands start to matter as much as the stopwatch.</p>
<h2 id="tightends">The Explosiveness Outliers</h2>
<p>Two tight ends redefined what's possible in the jumps this year. Vanderbilt's Eli Stowers broke combine records in both the vertical jump (45.5 inches) and broad jump (11 feet, 3 inches) — numbers that would be elite for a wide receiver, let alone a 250-pound tight end. Oregon's Kenyon Sadiq matched that explosiveness with a 4.39-second 40, a combine record at the position.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Player</th><th>Position</th><th>Combine Standout</th></tr>
<tr><td>Jeremiyah Love</td><td>RB</td><td>4.36s 40-yard dash</td></tr>
<tr><td>Brenen Thompson</td><td>WR</td><td>4.26s 40-yard dash</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eli Stowers</td><td>TE</td><td>45.5" vertical, 11'3" broad jump</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kenyon Sadiq</td><td>TE</td><td>4.39s 40-yard dash</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Vertical explosiveness at that level tends to show up first in contested-catch situations and red zone work, areas that don't get tested until pads and live reps arrive in camp. It's an early storyline worth tracking as both tight ends push for early playing time behind established starters.</p>
