<h2 id="takeaway">The Takeaway</h2>
<p>Combine numbers predict traits, not production. A 4.36 tells you a player has rare speed; it doesn't tell you whether he'll find the crease, track the ball, or hold up in pass protection. That gap between testing and translation is exactly what training camp exists to close, and it's why the next few weeks of camp reporting matter more than the February highlight reel. Check back as preseason games begin — that's when combine speed finally gets tested in live football.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Do fast 40 times at the combine actually predict NFL success?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Partially, and it depends heavily on position. For receivers and cornerbacks, top-end speed correlates reasonably well with early playing time. For running backs and tight ends, agility and contact balance often matter more than straight-line speed — a fast 40 opens doors but rarely guarantees production on its own.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Why did Jeremiyah Love run a 4.36 despite weighing 212 pounds?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Combine 40 times are typically slower as weight increases, since more mass generally means less relative speed. Love's time matched Jahmyr Gibbs's 2023 mark despite carrying 15 more pounds, which is part of why evaluators graded his testing so highly.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How rare is a 45-inch vertical jump for a tight end?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Extremely rare. The all-time combine vertical jump record across all positions is 46 inches, set by safety Gerald Sensabaugh in 2005. Eli Stowers's 45.5-inch jump at tight end is a position record and near the all-time high across every position at the combine.</div>
</div>
